 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>The bandit is 2,500 ft away, 150° target aspect. Are you offensive?</summary><p>Yes, if your angles and closure are under control. A target aspect over 90° means you are behind its 3/9 line, and 2,500 ft is inside the control zone's 2,000–4,000 ft range.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Why not stay in lead pursuit all the way in?</summary><p>Lead collapses range and builds closure. Use it for a shot or to close distance, then go to lag to control closure and AOT, or you risk an overshoot.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are at 450 KIAS in a T-45. What limits a maximum-performance pull?</summary><p>You are above the 410 KIAS corner, so the load limit: 7.33 G above 5,000 ft.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Where in a 4 G loop is the turn tightest?</summary><p>Over the top, where gravity adds to the pull: about 5 radial G against 3 at the bottom.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are 3,000 ft above the deck. How far nose-low can you go?</summary><p>About 30° by the 10% rule.</p></details>
 <details><summary>In a one-circle fight, does a higher turn rate win?</summary><p>No. One-circle is a radius fight: the smaller turn radius has the advantage.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You are closing fast, low angle off, about to overshoot. Which yo-yo?</summary><p>A high yo-yo: roll away from the bandit's plane of motion and pull up, trading airspeed for altitude to kill closure, then come back down behind it. A low-angle overshoot at range beats a high-angle one in close.</p></details>
 <details><summary>How much room does a T-45 need for a split-S at maximum performance?</summary><p>About 6,000–6,500 ft. Start one only with that much room above the deck.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <p id="source-p1289"><strong>[1] Naval Air Training Command, CNATRA P-1289 (Rev. 01-16), Flight Training Instruction, Basic Fighter Maneuvering (1v1), Section Engaged Maneuvering (2v1), T-45.</strong> Revision 3 dated 7 January 2016, Change 1 dated 3 March 2021; cleared for public release. <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/local/docs/pat-pubs/P-1289.pdf">Read the publication (cnatra.navy.mil)</a>. Inspected 24 October 2026: introduction p. xiii; PADS and kill criteria pp. 2-2, 2-5; training rules pp. 3-1–3-3; aerodynamics pp. 4-1–4-8; offensive BFM pp. 5-1–5-25 (revision 2 adds pp. 4-6, 5-13–5-21 and 5-23–5-25).</p>
 <p id="source-p1218"><strong>[2] US Navy flight training publication P-1218, Tactical Formation, (11-98) Original.</strong> Read as transcribed by Integrated Publishing: <a href="https://navyflightmanuals.tpub.com/P-1218/Low-Yo-Yo-P-12180038-38.htm">low yo-yo, p. 27</a> and <a href="https://navyflightmanuals.tpub.com/P-1218/High-Yo-Yo-P-12180039-39.htm">high yo-yo, p. 28</a> (navyflightmanuals.tpub.com). Inspected 24 October 2026. The transcription carries no issuing command or distribution statement, and its host is not yet one the repository's source review can read, so claims cited to it are unverified by that review.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/bfm-p1289-evidence.md">P-1289 evidence</a>.</p>
